<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Asakura clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1471 CE – 1573 CE (approx.) · capital Ichijodani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Asakura lords of Echizen, whose castle town of Ichijodani was a cultural centre until Oda Nobunaga destroyed it in 1573.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-toshikage">Asakura Toshikage</a>, a retainer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiba-clan">Shiba</a> governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echizen-province">Echizen</a>, used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Onin War</a> to take control of the province, seizing power from his masters around 1471. He built his seat in the narrow valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ichijodani">Ichijodani</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-toshikage">Toshikage</a> is credited with an early house code that urged merit over birth and ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to live in the castle town. Five generations of Asakura made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ichijodani">Ichijodani</a> a prosperous town that welcomed refugee courtiers, poets and scholars from war-torn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. The domain remained largely stable while neighbouring provinces fell into chaos.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asakura-yoshikage">Asakura Yoshikage</a> sheltered the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a> and allied with the Azai against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. After defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa">Anegawa</a> in 1570, Nobunaga invaded in 1573, burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ichijodani">Ichijodani</a> and forced Yoshikage to kill himself. Its excavated streets are now a historic site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/asakura-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Asakura clan domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_mitumori_mokkou.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1471 CE – 1573 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ichijodani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Asakura Toshikage, Asakura Yoshikage</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Asakura clan, Asakura of Echizen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1522&amp;focus=asakura-clan-domain" aria-label="Asakura clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8116 878 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Asakura clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8436 1134L8448 1163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8448 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8436 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8436 1134h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ichijodani. <a href="/atlas?year=1522&amp;focus=asakura-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

